<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dyrham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/577">577</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Deorham</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Deorham was fought in 577 at Dyrham, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> leaders Ceawlin and Cuthwine killed three British kings and took the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Cirencester</a> and Bath. The victory cut the Britons of the south-west off from those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and opened the lower Severn valley to English settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dyrham.jpg" alt="Battle of Dyrham" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Frankcom · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deorham_577AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The only evidence for the battle is a single annal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, in the common stock on which all its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> build, which was edited into its present form in the later 9th century. The earliest manuscript, the Parker Chronicle, names the three British kings killed as Coinmail, Condidan and Farinmail. Scholars agree that the place-name Deorham survives in Dyrham, on the Cotswold escarpment a few miles north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>, and that the three cities were the Roman Corinium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Glevum</a> and Aquae Sulis. Saint Aldate, a British bishop, is said to have been killed in the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The annal gives no details, and the accounts of the fighting are modern reconstructions. In 1929 the antiquarian Welbore St Clair Baddeley supposed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> seized the hill fort of Hinton Hill Camp near Dyrham, which commanded the Avon valley, forcing the Britons of the three towns to unite in a failed attempt to dislodge them. The military historian Lieutenant-Colonel Alfred Burne instead imagined Ceawlin advancing methodically towards the Severn, the British forces drawn up on two ridges with the fort behind them, and a last stand in the fort that would explain why none of the three leaders escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In an influential lecture of 1849 Edwin Guest presented the battle as the decisive blow that sealed the fate of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, a view that historians such as Frank Stenton, John Morris, H. P. R. Finberg and J. N. L. Myres maintained into the 1980s.</p><p>Patrick Sims-Williams then argued that the Chronicle shows no sign of being a contemporary record for the sixth century and may draw on oral tradition, perhaps a Welsh triad, or on later West Saxon claims against Mercia in the Severn valley. Other scholars hold that the weight given to the towns reflects the polities of the ninth and tenth centuries. Roger Collins and Barbara Yorke questioned the battle's importance and dating without denying it took place, and Guy Halsall wrote that the named kings may have fought at Dyrham, though it is much less likely that they ruled the three cities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Deorham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ceawlin, who ruled in the later sixth century and was listed by Bede among the kings who held authority over the southern English, expanded westwards; the Chronicle records his victory at the <b>Battle of Dyrham</b> in 577, which brought Gloucester, Cirencester and Bath under West Saxon control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hwicce took shape in Gloucestershire, Worcestershire and western Warwickshire after the West Saxon victory at <b>Dyrham</b> in 577 opened the region to English settlement.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-hwicce">Kingdom of the Hwicce</a></li></ul></section>
